steps.body:
A Result is a discriminated union with `ok` as its tag. Make it generic, so each function can say what it returns on success and what it returns on failure:

```typescript types
type Result<T, E> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E };
```

Two small helpers build each side. `never` is the type with no values, so a `Result<T, never>` can only be a success, and it fits any `Result<T, E>`. The same goes for `err` and `Result<never, E>`:

```typescript
type Result<T, E> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E };

function ok<T>(value: T): Result<T, never> {
  return { ok: true, value };
}

function err<E>(error: E): Result<never, E> {
  return { ok: false, error };
}

const result: Result<number, string> = ok(21);
if (result.ok) {
  console.log(result.value * 2); // 42
}
```

The payoff is in the signature. `parseQuantity(text: string): Result<number, string>` tells every caller that parsing can fail, and the checker won't let them read `value` until they've checked `ok`.

